  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 233 PM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nBroad cyclonic flow covers the northern tier of the CONUS with the \nupper trough axis pushing well east of the area into the Ohio River \nValley, while a ridge is taking shape over southern CA. At the \nsurface, high pressure is centered in the Upper Midwest with dry air \nadvection leading to dew points in the 30s and 40s alongside plenty \nof sunshine helping us reach the upper 60s to around 70 degrees. \nThis is a bit warmer and drier than previously forecast, and as such \ndecided to increase temperatures a few degrees for Saturday, closer \nto the ensemble mean rather than on the cooler end of guidance. In \nthe meantime, as the sfc ridge axis moves across the area tonight \ninto early Saturday, we should continue to see clear skies with \nlight winds. This along with wet soils from the recent rainfall \nwould point to at least some potential for fog, and RAP/HRRR \nforecast soundings around the area show a shallow sfc layer \nindicative of that for a few hours around sunrise. However, the \nposition of the sfc high would also favor continued dry air \nadvection and perhaps this is why guidance isn't overly excited \nabout fog. SREF probabilities are rather low, and the signal from \nHREF and REFS points toward more patchy fog which makes sense for \nthe valleys that can get cooler. Given the fact our dew points are \nlower this afternoon, that would mean lower crossover temperatures \nas well. So while some patchy fog is within the realm of \npossibility, current thinking is that it looks more confined to low-\nlying spots like river valleys.\n\nThe upper ridge to our west builds over the weekend into early next \nweek, leading to northwest flow aloft in our area. While ensemble \nclusters have small differences in the strength of that ridge, they \nare in agreement on this general pattern holding with perhaps some \nflattening of the ridge by the end of the week. No notable sources \nof lift are noted between now and the end of the forecast period, so \na dry pattern should give us a welcome break from the rainy last few \ndays. After a crisp Saturday morning starting out in the 40s, near \naverage temperatures prevail through Monday with highs in the low \n70s and lows in the 50s. We warm up slightly for the back half of \nnext week as highs reach the 80s for some.",
